1
00:00:01,703 --> 00:00:04,642
(What an Unexpected Moment in My Life)

2
00:00:04,643 --> 00:00:05,873
Nice to see you all!

3
00:00:06,943 --> 00:00:09,042
It's been a long time, Ji Hyeon.

4
00:00:09,043 --> 00:00:10,342
- It's been a while. - It's nice to see you.

5
00:00:10,343 --> 00:00:12,012
- It's nice to see you. - We last saw him on the field day.

6
00:00:12,013 --> 00:00:13,353
- Right. - That's right.

7
00:00:13,453 --> 00:00:15,522
- Have you been well? - I've been working hard.

8
00:00:15,523 --> 00:00:16,952
- Wait. Did you perform at an event? - Oh, my.

9
00:00:16,953 --> 00:00:18,153
- Well, today... - Look at your outfit.

10
00:00:18,493 --> 00:00:20,392
- You're dressed in Western clothes. - You're wearing a suit.

11
00:00:20,393 --> 00:00:21,423
Oh, my.

12
00:00:22,063 --> 00:00:23,492
This man in high spirits,

13
00:00:23,493 --> 00:00:26,433
Ji Hyeon, has spent a day with a special someone.

14
00:00:26,933 --> 00:00:28,403
Let's watch the footage to find out who it is.

15
00:00:30,463 --> 00:00:33,673
(With a Special Someone)

16
00:00:34,843 --> 00:00:38,743
(Ji Hyeon is heading somewhere while humming a song.)

17
00:00:41,883 --> 00:00:43,983
(Smiling)

18
00:00:47,353 --> 00:00:49,383
Seoul Express Bus Terminal.

19
00:00:49,583 --> 00:00:51,122
- A bus terminal? - That's a bus terminal.

20
00:00:51,123 --> 00:00:52,393
(Seoul Express Bus Terminal)

21
00:00:55,523 --> 00:00:56,593
(Surprised)

22
00:00:58,333 --> 00:00:59,363
This place is nice.

23
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:02,262
Christmas is nearing.

24
00:01:02,263 --> 00:01:04,132
- It's already the end of the year. - Christmas is nearing already?

25
00:01:04,133 --> 00:01:05,773
My gosh, it will soon be Christmas.

26
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:10,703
(Buses and people are moving busily at the terminal.)

27
00:01:13,013 --> 00:01:14,073
There it is.

28
00:01:17,543 --> 00:01:20,213
There it is. I'm here.

29
00:01:21,953 --> 00:01:24,692
(It's a package collection point from express buses.)

30
00:01:24,693 --> 00:01:26,152
My octopuses are transported...

31
00:01:26,153 --> 00:01:27,592
- on express buses these days. - That's right.

32
00:01:27,593 --> 00:01:29,293
- You always use that service. - Yes.

33
00:01:29,423 --> 00:01:30,923
- That's the fastest service. - Yes.

34
00:01:32,033 --> 00:01:33,193
Hello.

35
00:01:34,763 --> 00:01:38,033
(He meticulously fills out the registration form.)

36
00:01:38,973 --> 00:01:40,773
- Hello. - Hello.

37
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:42,302
So you're the recipient.

38
00:01:42,303 --> 00:01:44,072
- There are two packages. - Yes.

39
00:01:44,073 --> 00:01:45,172
- Give it to the worker. - Okay.

40
00:01:45,173 --> 00:01:46,342
Your packages are outside.

41
00:01:46,343 --> 00:01:48,183
- Okay. Thank you. - Bye.

42
00:01:48,283 --> 00:01:50,643
- Who sent you the packages? - My mother did.

43
00:01:50,943 --> 00:01:52,153
- From Mokpo? - Yes.

44
00:01:54,453 --> 00:01:56,153
- These two packages are yours. - Okay.

45
00:01:57,023 --> 00:01:58,493
(Lifting)

46
00:01:59,093 --> 00:02:00,253
I didn't know about this service.

47
00:02:00,653 --> 00:02:02,563
- That's the fastest. - It arrives on the same day.

48
00:02:02,793 --> 00:02:04,122
That's the best way to get fresh seafood, right?

49
00:02:04,123 --> 00:02:06,492
- They'd still be alive. - Yes. When I worked in Mokpo,

50
00:02:06,493 --> 00:02:08,933
I used to send the packages through express buses...

51
00:02:09,033 --> 00:02:10,833
for customers who wanted their seafood fresh.

52
00:02:11,203 --> 00:02:12,303
So you used to do that.

53
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:13,972
Wait, that's different from a courier service?

54
00:02:13,973 --> 00:02:16,672
- It's not. - It's the same-day...

55
00:02:16,673 --> 00:02:17,803
- delivery. - The same-day delivery.

56
00:02:18,043 --> 00:02:19,442
They just send the packages via bus.

57
00:02:19,443 --> 00:02:21,012
- Right. - They can send only the packages?

58
00:02:21,013 --> 00:02:22,043
- Yes. - They can.

59
00:02:24,583 --> 00:02:25,612
My gosh.

60
00:02:25,613 --> 00:02:26,982
(The boxes arrive by bus with the food still fresh.)

61
00:02:26,983 --> 00:02:28,253
They're heavy.

62
00:02:30,453 --> 00:02:31,653
Park Ji Hyeon.

63
00:02:32,223 --> 00:02:33,352
- Hello. - Gosh, the ladies.

64
00:02:33,353 --> 00:02:34,392
You're beautiful.

65
00:02:34,393 --> 00:02:35,723
- They clapped to greet him. - Thank you.

66
00:02:36,793 --> 00:02:37,862
Hello.

67
00:02:37,863 --> 00:02:39,763
(Like a friendly guy, he smiles back at them.)

68
00:02:41,533 --> 00:02:45,873
(He exits the terminal in a good mood.)

69
00:02:48,603 --> 00:02:49,873
Gosh, they're heavy.

70
00:02:51,443 --> 00:02:54,813
(He loads the boxes into his car.)

71
00:02:55,883 --> 00:02:58,053
What? Did you buy a car?

72
00:02:58,153 --> 00:02:59,853
It's a rental.

73
00:03:00,353 --> 00:03:01,423
- It's a rental? - I see.

74
00:03:01,653 --> 00:03:03,252
They're precious packages.

75
00:03:03,253 --> 00:03:04,823
I wanted to transport them safely to my house.

76
00:03:04,993 --> 00:03:06,252
How precious could they be...

77
00:03:06,253 --> 00:03:07,553
- that you rented a car? - You rented a car for them?

78
00:03:10,063 --> 00:03:11,962
My gosh, look at the fall foliage.

79
00:03:11,963 --> 00:03:13,632
(He sets out on a drive with precious packages.)

80
00:03:13,633 --> 00:03:15,433
The autumn leaves look beautiful.

81
00:03:15,603 --> 00:03:16,833
Autumn leaves.

82
00:03:18,473 --> 00:03:21,103
It's the Han River.

83
00:03:23,373 --> 00:03:24,572
(He continues to hum melodically.)

84
00:03:24,573 --> 00:03:25,973
I can tell you're excited.

85
00:03:26,713 --> 00:03:27,873
Are you going somewhere?

86
00:03:29,313 --> 00:03:31,183
- I had to do something. - I see.

87
00:03:31,553 --> 00:03:34,313
There's a series of speed bumps ahead.

88
00:03:36,153 --> 00:03:38,893
(I'm here.)

89
00:03:40,523 --> 00:03:41,623
My gosh.

90
00:03:44,793 --> 00:03:48,533
(He comes back home while carrying the boxes.)

91
00:03:49,963 --> 00:03:52,202
- Your house looks quite neat. - Right?

92
00:03:52,203 --> 00:03:53,772
Right? That thing is gone.

93
00:03:53,773 --> 00:03:55,473
- Hey, why is your house so clean? - Where are the humidifiers?

94
00:03:56,703 --> 00:03:59,743
(With a big humidifier in the middle of the living room,)

95
00:04:00,173 --> 00:04:02,613
(his house looked a bit chaotic.)

96
00:04:03,583 --> 00:04:05,813
Yes. You got rid of everything.

97
00:04:06,483 --> 00:04:09,352
I needed the counter for something since a few days ago.

98
00:04:09,353 --> 00:04:12,022
- I had to clear the counter first. - You needed a lot of space?

99
00:04:12,023 --> 00:04:13,053
- Yes. - I see.

100
00:04:14,053 --> 00:04:15,123
What?

101
00:04:16,263 --> 00:04:17,763
She sent me a knife too.

102
00:04:18,863 --> 00:04:20,963
- A knife? Oh, my. - My gosh.

103
00:04:21,093 --> 00:04:23,203
- Gosh, that's a sashimi knife. - It's a sashimi knife.

104
00:04:26,633 --> 00:04:28,372
My mother sent it to me because she wanted me to cut it nicely.

105
00:04:28,373 --> 00:04:30,373
Looking at your suit and the knife you're holding...

106
00:04:31,343 --> 00:04:32,442
- Right. - You could be...

107
00:04:32,443 --> 00:04:34,012
- in a noir film. - Right.

108
00:04:34,013 --> 00:04:35,243
- "Sunflower." - That's right.

109
00:04:35,513 --> 00:04:36,883
"Just spend three years in there."

110
00:04:37,743 --> 00:04:39,552
"This is great weather to slice sashimi."

111
00:04:39,553 --> 00:04:40,913
We might hear him sing "Wild Bee."

112
00:04:43,783 --> 00:04:45,723
He's good at cutting seafood.

113
00:04:47,153 --> 00:04:48,822
He used to work in that field.

114
00:04:48,823 --> 00:04:50,263
Yes. He looks professional.

115
00:04:52,233 --> 00:04:54,903
(Rummaging through)

116
00:04:59,133 --> 00:05:00,332
(Fresh octopus from Mokpo)

117
00:05:00,333 --> 00:05:01,642
- The three-legged ones? - Yes.

118
00:05:01,643 --> 00:05:03,102
- Aren't they expensive? - Yes, they are.

119
00:05:03,103 --> 00:05:04,443
- They're so expensive now. - She sent me a lot of abalones.

120
00:05:04,973 --> 00:05:06,843
- Oh, my. Look how big they are. - Abalones.

121
00:05:07,173 --> 00:05:08,783
- Are they from Wan Island? - Yes.

122
00:05:10,513 --> 00:05:11,783
Okay. I got this.

123
00:05:12,253 --> 00:05:15,082
If he's preparing fresh seafood, this someone must be very special.

124
00:05:15,083 --> 00:05:16,153
It could be his teacher.

125
00:05:17,583 --> 00:05:21,023
(As soon as he opens them up, he calls someone.)

126
00:05:22,723 --> 00:05:24,363
- Hello. - Hello.

127
00:05:24,663 --> 00:05:25,663
Hey.

128
00:05:26,163 --> 00:05:27,532
- Mom. - What are you doing?

129
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:28,603
- Yes. - I got the boxes.

130
00:05:28,903 --> 00:05:30,303
- You did? - Yes.

131
00:05:30,363 --> 00:05:31,703
Why did you send me salted shrimp?

132
00:05:32,173 --> 00:05:34,903
It's more fermented than I expected.

133
00:05:35,703 --> 00:05:36,973
When did you get this?

134
00:05:38,003 --> 00:05:39,443
I fermented it for about a week.

135
00:05:41,683 --> 00:05:43,943
It must weigh around 5kg.

136
00:05:44,243 --> 00:05:45,313
Yes. About that much.

137
00:05:45,483 --> 00:05:46,583
Okay then.

138
00:05:53,623 --> 00:05:55,163
- Is that a skate? - Yes.

139
00:05:55,663 --> 00:05:56,722
A skate? Gosh.

140
00:05:56,723 --> 00:05:59,193
(He has brought a whole skate from his hometown, Mokpo.)

141
00:06:00,533 --> 00:06:03,162
(He will prepare the fish in his house in Seoul.)

142
00:06:03,163 --> 00:06:04,663
It's quite fermented.

143
00:06:05,973 --> 00:06:07,473
(Massive)

144
00:06:08,543 --> 00:06:11,303
(Grabbing powerfully)

145
00:06:15,413 --> 00:06:19,253
To cook for someone really important,

146
00:06:19,453 --> 00:06:21,753
I got a lot of ingredients.

147
00:06:22,583 --> 00:06:25,653
He's a great senior singer who represents my hometown.

148
00:06:26,023 --> 00:06:27,293
He's the pride of Mokpo.

149
00:06:27,693 --> 00:06:29,892
He's the best singer...

150
00:06:29,893 --> 00:06:31,223
- Is it who I think it is? - in our industry.

151
00:06:31,393 --> 00:06:32,663
It's Nam Jin.

152
00:06:33,233 --> 00:06:34,292
- My dear, Nam Jin. - He will be on our show?

153
00:06:34,293 --> 00:06:35,333
Yes, Nam Jin.

154
00:06:35,603 --> 00:06:37,462
Gosh, he debuted in 1965.

155
00:06:37,463 --> 00:06:39,373
- He's Elvis Presley of Korea. - He's just amazing.

156
00:06:39,903 --> 00:06:42,302
- He's so cool. - He's really cool.

157
00:06:42,303 --> 00:06:44,342
("Empty Glass," released in 1982)

158
00:06:44,343 --> 00:06:46,813
- "Empty Glass" was great. - Look at that gaze! Oh, my.

159
00:06:48,483 --> 00:06:49,712
("Nam Jin Receives Silver Crown Order of Cultural Merit")

160
00:06:49,713 --> 00:06:50,913
("Nam Jin Still Influential as Ever, Receives Achievement Award")

161
00:06:51,283 --> 00:06:52,952
(An all-time legendary singer celebrating his 60th anniversary)

162
00:06:52,953 --> 00:06:54,413
- The 60th anniversary. - The 60th anniversary.

163
00:06:54,783 --> 00:06:57,383
(The biggest role model of Ji Hyeon who often sings his songs at home)

164
00:06:57,953 --> 00:07:00,293
- He's the sexiest man at that age. - Truly.

165
00:07:01,053 --> 00:07:02,962
The term, "teto-man," went viral lately.

166
00:07:02,963 --> 00:07:04,322
He's the very first teto-man.

167
00:07:04,323 --> 00:07:05,832
- He's a real man. - He's so manly.

168
00:07:05,833 --> 00:07:07,062
- Yes. - Totally.

169
00:07:07,063 --> 00:07:08,332
Personally, I'm a huge fan.

170
00:07:08,333 --> 00:07:10,203
- Me too. - "I'm a man." When he sings that,

171
00:07:10,533 --> 00:07:11,872
- it gives me so much energy. - Yes.

172
00:07:11,873 --> 00:07:13,102
Just watching him is enough.

173
00:07:13,103 --> 00:07:14,503
- Right. - I can feel his energy.

174
00:07:15,343 --> 00:07:17,443
Wait. I've met him once.

175
00:07:18,473 --> 00:07:19,573
"Radio Star."

176
00:07:20,373 --> 00:07:21,583
- You filmed with him? - Yes.

177
00:07:21,913 --> 00:07:25,152
I met him in front of the bathroom and said, "It's an honor, sir."

178
00:07:25,153 --> 00:07:27,183
- My stage name is difficult. - Yes.

179
00:07:27,753 --> 00:07:29,353
- He asked, "What was it?" - I said, "CODE..."

180
00:07:29,523 --> 00:07:30,982
- He said, "So you're a Ko?" - Okay.

181
00:07:30,983 --> 00:07:32,592
He told me I could tease him however much I wanted.

182
00:07:32,593 --> 00:07:33,692
- Right. - Yes.

183
00:07:33,693 --> 00:07:35,292
- He says that. - He told me to treat him...

184
00:07:35,293 --> 00:07:36,863
any other guest.

185
00:07:37,123 --> 00:07:39,032
Then he told me about how he got stabbed.

186
00:07:39,033 --> 00:07:40,263
(Then he told me about how he got stabbed.)

187
00:07:40,593 --> 00:07:42,032
He told you a very important story.

188
00:07:42,033 --> 00:07:43,263
That's an important story.

189
00:07:43,863 --> 00:07:45,872
- You're dressed like him today. - Yes.

190
00:07:45,873 --> 00:07:46,932
(Ji Hyeon is dressed just like him today.)

191
00:07:46,933 --> 00:07:49,103
- He likes three-piece suits. - Respect.

192
00:07:49,603 --> 00:07:52,842
- He always wears rings. - He likes wearing rings.

193
00:07:52,843 --> 00:07:55,182
- Gemstone rings? - He likes fancy accessories.

194
00:07:55,183 --> 00:07:56,283
Getting punched with that will hurt.

195
00:07:57,183 --> 00:08:00,453
I contacted him during the holidays.

196
00:08:00,983 --> 00:08:03,653
So I couldn't see him in person because of our schedules.

197
00:08:03,783 --> 00:08:06,323
Then he invited me to come over.

198
00:08:06,623 --> 00:08:08,423
But I can't show up empty-handed.

199
00:08:08,523 --> 00:08:10,122
I can't go and order food...

200
00:08:10,123 --> 00:08:11,733
- from a delivery app. - Right.

201
00:08:11,993 --> 00:08:13,933
To show him how much I respect him,

202
00:08:14,133 --> 00:08:16,462
the only thing I could think of was to bring the food with me.

203
00:08:16,463 --> 00:08:18,032
It will be like a royal meal...

204
00:08:18,033 --> 00:08:19,632
- for a king. - My gosh. Yes.

205
00:08:19,633 --> 00:08:20,672
That's great.

206
00:08:20,673 --> 00:08:22,702
He's an important part of my life.

207
00:08:22,703 --> 00:08:23,802
He means a lot to me.

208
00:08:23,803 --> 00:08:25,843
(To cook for someone he treasures...)

209
00:08:26,673 --> 00:08:27,973
I put my heart into it.

210
00:08:28,343 --> 00:08:29,782
- You look great as a court maid. - You look great.

211
00:08:29,783 --> 00:08:30,913
- You look beautiful. - I did my best.

212
00:08:31,243 --> 00:08:33,683
- Hey, they put effort into the CGI. - Hey, you look so pretty.

213
00:08:34,213 --> 00:08:35,753
I wanted him to have a taste of home.

214
00:08:36,323 --> 00:08:37,852
The packages had a skate, octopuses,

215
00:08:37,853 --> 00:08:40,193
abalones, and salted fish.

216
00:08:40,893 --> 00:08:41,893
That's it.

217
00:08:41,894 --> 00:08:45,193
I'm not cooking for myself. It's for my VIP.

218
00:08:45,463 --> 00:08:47,363
I made an effort to clean the kitchen.

219
00:08:47,933 --> 00:08:50,333
I wanted to cook for him and didn't want anything...

220
00:08:50,463 --> 00:08:51,973
to be a problem.

221
00:08:52,333 --> 00:08:53,772
Isn't it his 60th anniversary...

222
00:08:53,773 --> 00:08:55,203
- this year? - It is.

223
00:08:55,373 --> 00:08:56,643
- This is quite meaningful then. - Yes.

224
00:08:57,003 --> 00:08:58,672
A junior singer whose role model is him...

225
00:08:58,673 --> 00:09:00,042
- is cooking for him. - Right.

226
00:09:00,043 --> 00:09:01,072
They're from the same hometown.

227
00:09:01,073 --> 00:09:02,442
Then is he 80 years old?

228
00:09:02,443 --> 00:09:03,583
- He's older than 80. - He's 81.

229
00:09:03,783 --> 00:09:04,983
- Our age difference is 50 years. - He's amazing.

230
00:09:05,583 --> 00:09:07,213
- Isn't it exactly 50? - Yes.

231
00:09:07,483 --> 00:09:09,523
He was born in 1945, and I was born in 1995.

232
00:09:09,683 --> 00:09:10,853
- He was born in 1945? - Yes.

233
00:09:11,083 --> 00:09:13,093
- He experienced the Korean War. - Right.

234
00:09:13,293 --> 00:09:14,823
- He fought in the Vietnam War. - The Vietnam War.

235
00:09:15,063 --> 00:09:16,262
He's the living history.

236
00:09:16,263 --> 00:09:17,593
(He's indeed the living history of Korea.)

237
00:09:17,993 --> 00:09:19,962
I don't have a big cutting board.

238
00:09:19,963 --> 00:09:22,033
(He washes the cutting board and puts it down.)

239
00:09:24,333 --> 00:09:25,402
I tried to keep it clean.

240
00:09:25,403 --> 00:09:26,432
(He gets ready to cook in a clean environment for Nam Jin.)

241
00:09:26,433 --> 00:09:27,603
- You want this to be a treat. - Yes.

242
00:09:32,143 --> 00:09:34,073
(Let me start.)

243
00:09:36,143 --> 00:09:37,913
(Chopping)

244
00:09:40,683 --> 00:09:42,582
- Gosh, he's so good at this. - Oh, my.

245
00:09:42,583 --> 00:09:44,682
- He has the best knife skills. - It's "Culinary Class Wars."

246
00:09:44,683 --> 00:09:45,693
(With his knife skill, it looks like a cooking competition.)

247
00:09:49,493 --> 00:09:50,623
He's good.

248
00:09:51,263 --> 00:09:52,792
(Chopping furiously)

249
00:09:52,793 --> 00:09:54,833
To cook for someone really important to me...

250
00:09:56,163 --> 00:09:58,703
(He's chopping it wholeheartedly.)

251
00:10:01,003 --> 00:10:02,943
It's fun to watch him cook.

252
00:10:03,373 --> 00:10:04,473
He's good at cooking.

253
00:10:04,673 --> 00:10:07,043
He's like a professional. So it's fun to watch him cook.

254
00:10:09,213 --> 00:10:10,513
You finely chopped the vegetables.

255
00:10:11,883 --> 00:10:12,953
Abalones are fresh.

256
00:10:16,383 --> 00:10:17,923
The abalones are huge.

257
00:10:19,323 --> 00:10:20,423
Doesn't it feel nice?

258
00:10:22,163 --> 00:10:23,293
(Shucking the shell)

259
00:10:25,933 --> 00:10:26,992
He's good.

260
00:10:26,993 --> 00:10:27,993
(He prepares the abalones without any problem.)

261
00:10:31,003 --> 00:10:32,773
- What's that? - The innards of the abalones.

262
00:10:35,643 --> 00:10:37,373
(Grabbing something)

263
00:10:38,913 --> 00:10:40,813
(A mortar suddenly shows up.)

264
00:10:42,583 --> 00:10:43,742
(Pouring the innards)

265
00:10:43,743 --> 00:10:45,253
Into the mortar?

266
00:10:45,883 --> 00:10:47,513
(Circling)

267
00:10:48,523 --> 00:10:49,953
That's the origin of mortars.

268
00:10:50,283 --> 00:10:51,622
I don't have a blender.

269
00:10:51,623 --> 00:10:52,793
Using that is the authentic way.

270
00:10:53,023 --> 00:10:55,023
I ran everything in my head the day before.

271
00:10:57,093 --> 00:10:58,633
My gosh. Look at the color.

272
00:10:59,033 --> 00:11:00,563
It shows that he has experience.

273
00:11:00,963 --> 00:11:03,962
When you cook, you're not pretentious. I love that.

274
00:11:03,963 --> 00:11:06,373
- Who's pretentious when they cook? - What would be pretentious cooking?

275
00:11:06,433 --> 00:11:08,702
- I think I know what you mean. - Yes. Right?

276
00:11:08,703 --> 00:11:10,572
I know someone who overuses fancy tools.

277
00:11:10,573 --> 00:11:11,843
Who's that pretentious jerk?

278
00:11:12,373 --> 00:11:13,773
Who's that jerk?

279
00:11:14,673 --> 00:11:16,813
(I'm famous for having tools.)

280
00:11:16,913 --> 00:11:17,913
That startled me.

281
00:11:18,013 --> 00:11:19,082
It's not hot at all.

282
00:11:19,083 --> 00:11:21,483
(Cooking is also all about looking cool for Moomoo.)

283
00:11:21,613 --> 00:11:22,852
It's about the tools.

284
00:11:22,853 --> 00:11:23,882
- Right. - Exactly.

285
00:11:23,883 --> 00:11:25,692
- Tools aren't important. - They're not.

286
00:11:25,693 --> 00:11:28,052
- Ji Hyeon doesn't show off at all. - People should keep that in mind.

287
00:11:28,053 --> 00:11:29,493
- Exactly. - He's super cool.

288
00:11:30,993 --> 00:11:32,133
"He's super cool."

289
00:11:32,263 --> 00:11:33,833
(Chuckling bitterly)

290
00:11:34,233 --> 00:11:36,463
(Putting down the pot in a super cool manner)

291
00:11:37,603 --> 00:11:39,232
I guess he's going to cook porridge.

292
00:11:39,233 --> 00:11:40,303
- That's right. - I see.

293
00:11:43,143 --> 00:11:45,613
(He tastes the abalone porridge with innards.)

294
00:11:46,513 --> 00:11:49,283
(Okay. I just have to boil this now.)

295
00:11:50,243 --> 00:11:52,383
Oh, no. My cutting board is too small.

296
00:11:54,383 --> 00:11:56,722
The cutting board is too small.

297
00:11:56,723 --> 00:11:57,753
He's being pretentious.

298
00:11:58,593 --> 00:11:59,593
That's cute.

299
00:11:59,753 --> 00:12:00,952
- He's just excited. - What's wrong with him?

300
00:12:00,953 --> 00:12:02,263
- I was just excited. - Yes.

301
00:12:03,193 --> 00:12:05,292
(He excitedly makes sure it's clean for Nam Jin.)

302
00:12:05,293 --> 00:12:06,733
It's been a while.

303
00:12:07,533 --> 00:12:08,633
Mr. Kim Skate.

304
00:12:09,263 --> 00:12:10,762
Are you fermented nicely?

305
00:12:10,763 --> 00:12:12,273
(Yes)

306
00:12:12,933 --> 00:12:14,403
My gosh. How are you going to cut that?

307
00:12:15,273 --> 00:12:17,672
- The cutting board is too small. - Yes. I didn't think about that.

308
00:12:17,673 --> 00:12:19,213
(It's bigger than the cutting board.)

309
00:12:19,513 --> 00:12:20,712
(Royal Meal from Mokpo for Someone Special)

310
00:12:20,713 --> 00:12:22,443
(A week-fermented skate)

311
00:12:25,013 --> 00:12:27,083
Oh, my. It really has been a while.

312
00:12:29,353 --> 00:12:31,053
(My knife is going in now.)

313
00:12:31,753 --> 00:12:33,523
(Slicing)

314
00:12:35,923 --> 00:12:36,963
(Putting it down)

315
00:12:37,293 --> 00:12:39,793
(He starts with the middle part.)

316
00:12:40,493 --> 00:12:42,062
- He's separating the liver. - My gosh.

317
00:12:42,063 --> 00:12:43,102
- Oh, my. - That's the liver.

318
00:12:43,103 --> 00:12:44,662
The gallbladder is in the middle.

319
00:12:44,663 --> 00:12:47,132
If you pop it while cutting the fish,

320
00:12:47,133 --> 00:12:48,702
- the fish gets covered in that. - You can't use the fish then.

321
00:12:48,703 --> 00:12:49,873
- Right. It's bitter then. - I see.

322
00:12:50,173 --> 00:12:52,513
My hands seem to remember what to do.

323
00:12:53,443 --> 00:12:55,013
That's the meat from the tail.

324
00:12:56,743 --> 00:12:58,412
Why did you put them in separate bowls?

325
00:12:58,413 --> 00:13:01,113
I separate the innards, bones, and flesh.

326
00:13:01,353 --> 00:13:02,853
- I use them separately. - I see.

327
00:13:03,383 --> 00:13:04,582
This must be completely different...

328
00:13:04,583 --> 00:13:05,792
- from regular fish. - Right.

329
00:13:05,793 --> 00:13:07,493
Preparing skate is such a big challenge.

330
00:13:08,123 --> 00:13:10,263
You need to execute that step well.

331
00:13:10,663 --> 00:13:13,393
Your knife must follow the bones next to the gill to cut it neatly.

332
00:13:16,763 --> 00:13:17,763
(Chopping)

333
00:13:19,633 --> 00:13:20,772
He's good.

334
00:13:20,773 --> 00:13:21,973
That's how a professional does it.

335
00:13:22,973 --> 00:13:24,643
- The wings. - Oh, my.

336
00:13:26,743 --> 00:13:28,743
(Cracking)

337
00:13:29,383 --> 00:13:30,942
- You're really good at this. - Yes.

338
00:13:30,943 --> 00:13:32,752
- That's a skill too. - What are you doing?

339
00:13:32,753 --> 00:13:34,412
That's the cheek.

340
00:13:34,413 --> 00:13:35,883
The cheek meat is a rare cut.

341
00:13:37,823 --> 00:13:39,353
You don't hesitate at all.

342
00:13:40,223 --> 00:13:41,293
(He continues on.)

343
00:13:41,493 --> 00:13:43,393
- He's really good. - He's so good.

344
00:13:43,623 --> 00:13:46,063
That's where the mouth is. I cut the gill in half.

345
00:13:46,533 --> 00:13:48,232
Here's the thing with the gills.

346
00:13:48,233 --> 00:13:49,462
You must rinse them thoroughly with water.

347
00:13:49,463 --> 00:13:51,332
- Otherwise, they'd stink a lot. - I see.

348
00:13:51,333 --> 00:13:52,532
Is it like having a bad breath?

349
00:13:52,533 --> 00:13:53,933
Yes, it's something like that.

350
00:13:54,233 --> 00:13:56,103
- They breathe through that. - Right.

351
00:13:56,303 --> 00:13:58,173
(Splitting the gill open in half)

352
00:13:58,243 --> 00:13:59,313
My gosh.

353
00:13:59,843 --> 00:14:01,513
It's been three years.

354
00:14:01,613 --> 00:14:04,542
I was 23 years old when I cut my 1st skate.

355
00:14:04,543 --> 00:14:05,553
That's when I started working.

356
00:14:05,653 --> 00:14:07,513
Then I worked until I was 29.

357
00:14:08,113 --> 00:14:10,352
- I cut at least 30 skate every day. - My gosh.

358
00:14:10,353 --> 00:14:11,582
He said 30 skate every day.

359
00:14:11,583 --> 00:14:12,693
I did that for seven years.

360
00:14:13,493 --> 00:14:14,952
So 300 working days in 1 year...

361
00:14:14,953 --> 00:14:16,393
Three times seven is twenty-one.

362
00:14:16,763 --> 00:14:18,363
So for 2,100 days...

363
00:14:19,033 --> 00:14:21,262
I cut 30 skate every day for 2,100 days.

364
00:14:21,263 --> 00:14:22,303
Oh, my.

365
00:14:22,333 --> 00:14:23,333
Two times three is six.

366
00:14:23,334 --> 00:14:24,932
- So it's around 60,000. - I cut 6,300 skate.

367
00:14:24,933 --> 00:14:26,272
(He cut 6,300 skate in 7 years?)

368
00:14:26,273 --> 00:14:28,202
Is that right? Is it 6,300 skate?

369
00:14:28,203 --> 00:14:29,542
- Isn't it around 60,000 skate? - Isn't it 60,000 skate?

370
00:14:29,543 --> 00:14:30,772
It's 63,000?

371
00:14:30,773 --> 00:14:32,173
Gosh, 63,000 skate?

372
00:14:33,273 --> 00:14:34,712
(Shocked)

373
00:14:34,713 --> 00:14:37,143
Gosh, see? He's not showing off.

374
00:14:37,483 --> 00:14:38,883
He didn't calculate it until now.

375
00:14:39,553 --> 00:14:42,253
- He's good. - That's being genuine.

376
00:14:42,353 --> 00:14:45,183
So many chefs these days aren't genuine.

377
00:14:45,653 --> 00:14:46,952
- Like who? - Who do you mean?

378
00:14:46,953 --> 00:14:47,953
Never mind.

379
00:14:49,263 --> 00:14:50,562
He always looks at me then.

380
00:14:50,563 --> 00:14:52,963
No. Well, you happen to be there.

381
00:14:54,033 --> 00:14:56,063
(A certain someone who often uses fancy tools)

382
00:14:58,063 --> 00:15:00,603
(He cuts the skate genuinely from the bottom of his heart.)

383
00:15:02,703 --> 00:15:04,243
(He uses his hand to pull it.)

384
00:15:05,513 --> 00:15:07,412
- He's peeling off the skin. - My gosh.

385
00:15:07,413 --> 00:15:08,683
(He tugs the skin to peel it.)

386
00:15:10,943 --> 00:15:12,883
(Slicing it once)

387
00:15:13,613 --> 00:15:14,853
My gosh.

388
00:15:15,323 --> 00:15:16,423
He's amazing.

389
00:15:16,753 --> 00:15:18,583
I can't believe he's doing that at home.

390
00:15:18,723 --> 00:15:20,493
Do you want to compete in "Culinary Class Wars?"

391
00:15:20,593 --> 00:15:21,723
No.

392
00:15:23,193 --> 00:15:25,433
(He also removes pieces of skin on the fish.)

393
00:15:28,593 --> 00:15:30,633
(Only the skin is removed, without a piece of flesh.)

394
00:15:31,833 --> 00:15:33,633
It's like he's at a sashimi restaurant.

395
00:15:34,833 --> 00:15:36,503
(He grabs something.)

396
00:15:38,343 --> 00:15:39,443
The thawing sheets.

397
00:15:39,613 --> 00:15:41,043
Gosh, you used thawing sheets too?

398
00:15:44,313 --> 00:15:46,613
I call the process sweating out the fish.

399
00:15:46,853 --> 00:15:48,483
When the fish is wrapped up like that briefly,

400
00:15:48,653 --> 00:15:49,952
- Right. - it's chewier.

401
00:15:49,953 --> 00:15:51,083
Sweating out the fish.

402
00:15:52,653 --> 00:15:54,193
(Grabbing)

403
00:15:56,163 --> 00:15:58,593
(For his senior singer who debuted 50 years before him,)

404
00:16:01,793 --> 00:16:03,963
(he will dust off his skills from the old days.)

405
00:16:06,103 --> 00:16:07,872
(Chopping)

406
00:16:07,873 --> 00:16:09,843
My gosh. That looks tasty.

407
00:16:10,473 --> 00:16:12,413
(Cutting a slice of skate)

408
00:16:13,413 --> 00:16:15,013
(Tasting)

409
00:16:16,943 --> 00:16:18,513
(It's good.)

410
00:16:19,553 --> 00:16:22,523
(Cutting it for the satisfying crunch)

411
00:16:23,883 --> 00:16:25,553
Is it supposed to be crunchy?

412
00:16:25,693 --> 00:16:27,423
- Yes. - Yes. It has bones inside.

413
00:16:29,063 --> 00:16:30,263
That looks great.

414
00:16:31,963 --> 00:16:33,933
(Opening)

415
00:16:40,503 --> 00:16:42,803
(He puts on gloves in the middle of cooking.)

416
00:16:44,473 --> 00:16:46,412
(The snout is the finale.)

417
00:16:46,413 --> 00:16:47,743
Look at that.

418
00:16:48,113 --> 00:16:50,243
(As it's slippery, he puts on gloves.)

419
00:16:50,813 --> 00:16:52,613
(The snout of the skate)

420
00:16:54,213 --> 00:16:55,853
Gosh, that's the stinkiest part.

421
00:16:56,383 --> 00:16:57,552
There are different groups for skate eaters.

422
00:16:57,553 --> 00:16:59,193
- Groups that eat snouts and don't. - Right.

423
00:16:59,353 --> 00:17:02,263
Right. I want to put some aged kimchi on that.

424
00:17:04,223 --> 00:17:05,933
(Next up is a halved chunk of meat.)

425
00:17:09,733 --> 00:17:11,762
(Adding the whole chunk into the pot)

426
00:17:11,763 --> 00:17:12,903
He's making suyuk.

427
00:17:13,173 --> 00:17:14,372
- For the skate combo. - Yes.

428
00:17:14,373 --> 00:17:16,243
- He must prepare the skate combo. - Yes.

429
00:17:19,973 --> 00:17:21,012
Oh, my.

430
00:17:21,013 --> 00:17:22,513
(He even brings out beef.)

431
00:17:25,213 --> 00:17:26,553
Is it for beef radish soup?

432
00:17:26,683 --> 00:17:28,052
- Okay. - I see.

433
00:17:28,053 --> 00:17:29,423
He's making radish soup.

434
00:17:29,623 --> 00:17:30,682
He makes it look so easy.

435
00:17:30,683 --> 00:17:32,123
Okay.

436
00:17:32,953 --> 00:17:33,992
Come here.

437
00:17:33,993 --> 00:17:35,562
- Thanks for making the long trip. - Octopus.

438
00:17:35,563 --> 00:17:36,723
Oh, my.

439
00:17:37,393 --> 00:17:39,633
Thank you for coming all the way here.

440
00:17:40,893 --> 00:17:42,302
It will be a stamina-boosting meal.

441
00:17:42,303 --> 00:17:43,903
(He acquires only the delicacies like skate, beef, and octopus.)

442
00:17:44,803 --> 00:17:46,573
I don't want this to choke him.

443
00:17:49,543 --> 00:17:50,573
Chopped raw octopus?

444
00:17:50,903 --> 00:17:53,773
- Yes. He must chop that. - All right.

445
00:17:55,243 --> 00:17:56,283
What's that?

446
00:17:57,213 --> 00:17:58,983
(Sizzling)

447
00:18:00,213 --> 00:18:01,712
Gosh, you're cooking the live octopus?

448
00:18:01,713 --> 00:18:02,782
(He'll stir-fry this precious live octopus?)

449
00:18:02,783 --> 00:18:04,552
- You'll stir-fry the live octopus? - Yes.

450
00:18:04,553 --> 00:18:05,892
Now, you're just showing off.

451
00:18:05,893 --> 00:18:07,492
I added abalones to the stir-fried octopus too.

452
00:18:07,493 --> 00:18:09,163
My gosh. That's a top-quality dish.

453
00:18:12,593 --> 00:18:14,532
How many dishes is he cooking simultaneously?

454
00:18:14,533 --> 00:18:15,933
I cooked 6 to 7 dishes.

455
00:18:17,063 --> 00:18:18,763
He's being really generous.

456
00:18:20,203 --> 00:18:21,802
All right. Come here, suyuk.

457
00:18:21,803 --> 00:18:22,843
(Let me check on the suyuk.)

458
00:18:24,403 --> 00:18:26,313
Is it cooked or what?

459
00:18:28,673 --> 00:18:31,543
(The knife goes easily into the meat.)

460
00:18:32,283 --> 00:18:33,913
(It's cooked perfectly.)

461
00:18:34,953 --> 00:18:36,452
(Adding to the insulated container)

462
00:18:36,453 --> 00:18:37,953
- He's doing a great job. - That's great.

463
00:18:38,253 --> 00:18:40,023
He's good at adding the food into that tiny box.

464
00:18:41,253 --> 00:18:42,763
(Opening)

465
00:18:43,023 --> 00:18:44,322
(Aged kimchi for the suyuk)

466
00:18:44,323 --> 00:18:46,363
- It's aged kimchi. - Oh, dear. It's aged kimchi.

467
00:18:47,393 --> 00:18:48,933
(Whining)

468
00:18:50,003 --> 00:18:51,433
(Stained with the sauce)

469
00:18:53,373 --> 00:18:55,403
(I should wipe it off before he tastes it.)

470
00:18:56,203 --> 00:18:57,703
(Suyuk and kimchi, the perfect combo for skate)

471
00:18:58,973 --> 00:19:01,243
(Thick abalone porridge with innards)

472
00:19:01,443 --> 00:19:02,642
You used insulated containers...

473
00:19:02,643 --> 00:19:03,883
- for everything. - Yes.

474
00:19:04,043 --> 00:19:05,442
It was quite far.

475
00:19:05,443 --> 00:19:07,412
Right. Temperature is important in cooking.

476
00:19:07,413 --> 00:19:08,883
Especially so for elders.

477
00:19:09,053 --> 00:19:10,082
Their food must be nice and warm.

478
00:19:10,083 --> 00:19:11,222
- Right. - Temperature is important.

479
00:19:11,223 --> 00:19:12,353
(Refreshing beef radish soup)

480
00:19:13,253 --> 00:19:15,922
(Salted octopus, a representative side dish of Mokpo)

481
00:19:15,923 --> 00:19:18,163
Salted hairtail intestines look so tasty!

482
00:19:19,163 --> 00:19:21,193
(Nam Jin's favorite dish, green onion kimchi)

483
00:19:22,093 --> 00:19:24,963
- That's a lot of love. - You prepared all this for him?

484
00:19:25,303 --> 00:19:28,073
It will take me about eight hours to prepare this dish.

485
00:19:28,233 --> 00:19:29,542
How long did it take to cook everything?

486
00:19:29,543 --> 00:19:32,442
I could've finished sooner.

487
00:19:32,443 --> 00:19:34,472
I also had to pack up the food.

488
00:19:34,473 --> 00:19:35,882
- It took about two hours. - Gosh!

489
00:19:35,883 --> 00:19:37,542
- He cooks really fast. - That's really fast.

490
00:19:37,543 --> 00:19:38,612
He broke down the skate...

491
00:19:38,613 --> 00:19:39,682
- from scratch. - Right.

492
00:19:39,683 --> 00:19:41,182
- He's really good. - He's fast.

493
00:19:41,183 --> 00:19:42,213
Right.

494
00:19:43,523 --> 00:19:45,953
(Thinking)

495
00:19:47,053 --> 00:19:49,163
(It's missing something.)

496
00:19:51,723 --> 00:19:52,792
(Figs?)

497
00:19:52,793 --> 00:19:54,332
- They're figs. - Figs.

498
00:19:54,333 --> 00:19:55,362
I had them before.

499
00:19:55,363 --> 00:19:57,133
- We tried them before. - They were so good.

500
00:19:58,263 --> 00:20:01,833
(He brought figs on Innocent Moo-Ku Fall Field Day.)

501
00:20:02,873 --> 00:20:06,373
(His figs won over the hearts of the members at once.)

502
00:20:07,313 --> 00:20:08,572
- They were so sweet. - They were so good.

503
00:20:08,573 --> 00:20:09,642
- Figs are the best. - They were different...

504
00:20:09,643 --> 00:20:10,782
from the ones I was used to.

505
00:20:10,783 --> 00:20:13,052
- Right? They're fuller inside. - They tasted different.

506
00:20:13,053 --> 00:20:14,113
- Right. - Yes.

507
00:20:14,513 --> 00:20:16,483
(He inspects and picks out only the pretty ones.)

508
00:20:16,983 --> 00:20:19,193
(Carefully rinsing them)

509
00:20:19,323 --> 00:20:21,293
How beautiful.

510
00:20:23,623 --> 00:20:25,422
Did you pick out only the best ones?

511
00:20:25,423 --> 00:20:26,732
- Right. Yes, only the pretty ones. - Only the pretty ones?

512
00:20:26,733 --> 00:20:28,193
- Not the popped ones, right? - Right.

513
00:20:29,333 --> 00:20:31,963
(A complete royal meal with a dessert)

514
00:20:33,033 --> 00:20:34,202
All done.

515
00:20:34,203 --> 00:20:35,273
(A generous amount of food)

516
00:20:36,103 --> 00:20:37,973
(He opens up the cabinet again when he's done preparing the food.)

517
00:20:39,913 --> 00:20:41,873
- What's that? A wrapper too? - You're going to wrap them up?

518
00:20:42,883 --> 00:20:45,383
Rock-wrapper-scissors.

519
00:20:46,183 --> 00:20:47,683
I thought I misheard you.

520
